    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class in the project, and it passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` field on the `POST /api/owners` endpoint, returning the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same firstName and lastName, case-insensitively.\n\nFollowing the OfficeFloor function-injection pattern (mirroring the existing `AssignCustomerCode` step):\n\n1. **`CountNamesakes.java`** (new function) \u2014 reads the built `Owner` via `@Val`, counts existing owners from `ownerRepository.findAll()` whose firstName and lastName match case-insensitively, and stores the result with `owner.setNamesakeCount(...)`. It runs before `SaveOwner`, so the new owner isn't counted.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `namesakes` step (with `govern: [ transaction ]`) between `customerCode` and `save`.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `Integer namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter, so `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns it.\n\n4. **Schema files** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column (ddl-auto is `none`, so schema must be explicit).\n\n5. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gets the field and MapStruct auto-maps it by name.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreCountsNamesakes` (two owners named \"Ann\" \u2192 second returns `namesakeCount = 1`) and the full suite pass.",
